---
import Image from "astro/components/Image.astro";

import on from "../content/posts/images/on.jpg";
import fyr from "../content/posts/images/fyr.jpg";
import fyrBack from "../content/posts/images/fyr-back.jpg";
import debrisSides from "../content/posts/images/debris-sides.jpg";
---

<div class="bg-black text-gray-100 py-4">
  <div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-2 max-w-7xl px-4 mx-auto my-8">
    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl">Ön</h2>
    </div>
    <Image src={on} alt="hero image" loading={"eager"} />
  </div>
</div>

<div
  class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4 max-w-4xl px-4 mx-auto my-8 text-sm border-b border-gray-100 pt-4 pb-8"
>
  <div class="grid">
    <Image src={debrisSides} alt="hero image" loading={"eager"} />
  </div>

  <div class="col-span-2 md:col-span-1">
    <h2 class="text-base">DEBRIS SIDES</h2>
    <div>
      <p>2024</p>
      <br />
      <p>Bandcamp</p>
      <p>Spotify</p>
      <br />
      <p>
        In October 2011 we released our first full-length album 'Debris'.
        Someone close passed away two years earlier and music and writing
        quickly became very important in dealing with that. I wanted to document
        it in some way, even if it was confused, broken and just fragmented
        thoughts moving in circles. Just thoughts drifting like debris.
        <br />
        <br />
        We wrote and recorded a lot and ended up with more songs than we could fit
        in one album. So some of them had to sit back and wait.
        <br />
        <br />
        In October 2023 - for the album’s 12 year anniversary - we went back to listen
        to these songs. And we instantly felt that these songs deserve to outlive
        the dying hard drive which was their home for so long.
        <br /><br />
        'Debris Sides' is possibly more confused, broken and fragmented than its
        12 year old sibling, but also more direct. Everything is out in the open,
        for better or for worse.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <!-- <div>
      <ol>
        <li>Saida</li>
        <li>På älven</li>
        <li>Tuvåkra</li>
        <li>Pappa</li>
        <li>Genom skärvorna</li>
        <li>Signal</li>
        <li>Vem kan segla förutan vind?</li>
        <li>Altappen</li>
        <li>Till Slut</li>
      </ol>
      <div></div>
    </div> -->
</div>

<div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 max-w-4xl px-4 mx-auto my-8 text-sm">
  <Image src={fyr} alt="album cover of fyr" />
  <Image src={fyrBack} alt="backside of album cover fyr" />
  <div class="col-span-2 md:col-span-1">
    <h2 class="text-base">FYR</h2>
    <div>
      <p>Whitelabrecs 2023</p>
      <br />
      <p>Bandcamp</p>
      <p>Spotify</p>
      <br />
      <p>
        Recorded between February and April, 2023. Our third instrumental album
        and very much the spiritual successor to Taiga, which was released on
        Whitelabrecs 2019. <br />
        <br /> Fyr is a collection of memories suspended in time. It is the overgrown
        park, the written word on the demolished building, the pulsating light of
        a dead star, the tiny fragments of color breaking through the concrete, the
        fear that love isn’t enough, the silence after the machines stopped. It is
        to save these places and people from fading into oblivion. It was inspired
        by the words of Tove Jansson, the nameless voices on the shortwave radio,
        and the sounds of The Beach Boys. <br />
        <br /> Written, recorded and mixed by Old Amica. Mastered by James Edward
        Armstrong. Photography by J.M. Almqvist and Yuliana Paranko.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
